  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Is Cement Board Alone Waterproof for a Bathroom Shower?",
    "text": "Is Cement Board Alone Waterproof for a Bathroom Shower?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Many homeowners assume that installing cement backer board on shower studs is all that is needed before setting tile. However, standard cement board is moisture-resistant, not waterproof. While water will not cause cement board to rot or disintegrate like drywall, moisture easily passes right through porous cementitious boards into the wall cavity, where wooden studs and insulation can develop rot and mold.\n\nWhat to Expect During the Process\n\nA safe, durable shower installation requires a dedicated, continuous waterproofing membrane installed over or behind the backer board:\n- In bonded systems, a liquid-applied or sheet membrane complying with ANSI A118.10 covers the face of the cement board, sealing seams, screws, and pipe penetrations before tile is set.\n- Traditional assemblies require a vapor-retarding membrane placed behind the backer units to channel incidental moisture downward.\n- Grout and tile alone will never stop water penetration; they are porous materials that shed surface spray while letting moisture seep through microscopic cracks.\n- Corners, benches, and niches must be taped with alkali-resistant mesh and fully sealed with compatible waterproofing compounds.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\n\nIf you are replacing an old shower or converting a tub, verifying backing flatness, proper drain integration, and slope toward the drain is critical. At HH Remodeling LLC, owner Abraham Becerra leads finish and tile work directly across Aurora and the greater Denver metro area. Call 303-505-0866 or reach out through https://www.coloradoremodeling.company to discuss your bathroom renovation.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
